WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.864
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.864 --> 00:00:04.830
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.690
I'm Dustin Kirkland
here a KubeCon.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.360
And I'm joined by Lakshmi
Sharma, Director of Product

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.860
Management at Google.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.050
Her team works on some
of the hardest network

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.599
problems at Google.

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:16.140
Lakshmi, can you
tell us a little bit

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.530
about what it takes to
think about networking

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.610
at tremendous scale inside
of GCP and with Kubernetes?

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.540
LAKSHMI SHARMA: Sure.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.480
So a lot of work that we do
in networking-- to be honest,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.570
this has been done
over 10, 12 years.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.040
We all know that
Kubernetes really

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.530
started from the intra
search and management

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.050
right inside Google's
data center like bulk.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.660
And so most of the networking
pieces that we have,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.690
they really run all those
applications at scale,

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:45.360
whether we talk about global
load balancer, internal load

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.830
balancers, and then doing that
at the geographical scales

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.510
at which we support
already 4 billion users,

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.590
4 billion containers every week.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.170
So that's a kind
of problem we have,

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:58.620
to be honest, solve at the
connectivity level, DDoS

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.840
level, and all of that, but
now, taking that problem

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.040
and trying to solve the problem
for our enterprise customers

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.960
so that they can get
that kind of scale

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.770
for business just like
we at Google have solved.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.590
So that's a problem we are
solving at this point--

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.140
how do we enable our
enterprise customers

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.560
to be able to manage
their business

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.350
and focus there and by
offering manage GKE--

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:25.220
manage GKE networking?

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.500
And recently, we just did an
alpha launch for something

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.370
called [? NEG, ?] network and
[? growing ?] groups and things

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:33.000
like that.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.840
And all these offerings are
going to enable our enterprises

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.430
to get managed services.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.490
And they can get a
seamless networking

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.810
from Google and BNGKEGCP.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:45.487
So I'm biased.

00:01:45.487 --> 00:01:47.070
I will talk about
Google networking so

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:47.820
what we have done.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.120
We are taking GKE ingress
to a very high scale

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.130
using Google and
Google Store balancers

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.655
and integrating them
into GKE ingress.

00:01:58.655 --> 00:02:00.960
That will give you the
large scale of networking

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.720
and security, SSL
proxies, all that you

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.480
need to secure your perimeter.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.100
And then through the same GKE
ingress, same load balancers,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.700
you can get the native load
balances for containers.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.760
If you still want to
continue to use NGNX,

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.510
you can use, if you
want to still continue

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.520
to use your choice
of load balancers.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.460
But if you want the
scale of the entire globe

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.650
and then being able to use
the Edge and the services

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:29.760
that we have, GKE ingress
with our load balancers.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.180
Those are the problems we solve.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:34.826
And we want enterprises to
be free of those problems.

00:02:34.826 --> 00:02:36.450
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So
you've talked a lot

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.550
about ingress and egress here.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.940
Can you tie this back to Istio
and some of the networking

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.520
challenges that you and
your team are solving

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.705
and how that fits in
with the Istio project?

00:02:46.705 --> 00:02:47.640
LAKSHMI SHARMA: Sure.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.810
So Istio, known as Service
Mesh, and the control

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.480
plane for different
kinds of proxies,

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.660
let's talk about ingress,
middle, and the side car proxy.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.150
And sitting either with
something like Envoy

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.070
or- in case of
Google, integrated

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:03.760
with Google's load balancers.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:04.710
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
And can you tell me

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:06.293
a little bit about
what you're hearing

00:03:06.293 --> 00:03:10.980
from enterprise customers in
terms of multi-cloud demands

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:11.790
on networking?

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.831
LAKSHMI SHARMA: So I'll
talk about two categories

00:03:13.831 --> 00:03:14.470
of customers.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.350
So one is just the
cloud native customers.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.380
Most of our initial
customers were really

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.100
cloud native customers.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.400
And then various
other cloud providers,

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.310
they had initially
customers onboarded,

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.380
which were traditional
enterprise ID customers.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:31.080
So I'll pick both
kinds of customers.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.060
So enterprise ID
customers, they are

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.530
hybrid from the perspective of
on prem and some public cloud,

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:39.600
or they may have some
instances of private cloud.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.160
So they're hybrid that way.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:45.900
And cloud native companies,
relatively newer companies,

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.490
gaming companies--

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.360
Pokemon being one of them.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.290
So those companies, they
were just born and brought up

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:53.370
in cloud.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.410
So then those other
companies, which

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.790
we have most of
experiences with,

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:59.880
so even those
companies, in order

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.230
to get some data locality, those
kinds of rules and regulations,

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:07.350
we see them also going to some
multi-cloud kind of scenarios

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.930
just primarily for
the data scenarios.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.550
But anytime you move
data, you end up

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.540
being in the networking
space as well.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.140
So that's we are seeing
traditional IT companies.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.000
They going on prem, off prem.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.669
And then they want a choice
of another public cloud.

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:22.700
But for cloud native
companies, they

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.560
started with one cloud
vendor, like let's say GCP.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:29.100
And we also see them going to
other cloud providers because

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.682
of some compliance reasons
or data localities.

00:04:32.682 --> 00:04:34.140
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
And what would you

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.990
say the hardest
problem is that we're

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:40.080
working on right now
from a Google perspective

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.160
on networking in the
cloud native space?

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.545
LAKSHMI SHARMA: So
hardest problem,

00:04:45.545 --> 00:04:46.670
nothing is hard for Google.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:48.530
We are really smart and genius.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.005
[LAUGHS]

00:04:50.005 --> 00:04:52.520
So I'd say from a
privatization perspective

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.310
and what do we want to
do in an upcoming year.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:02.750
So we have two cloud
providers, very famous ones.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:05.860
So they have a huge base of
large enterprise customers.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.360
And we are really good with
cloud native companies.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:12.130
So looking at sudden
verticals and I'd

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:16.240
say retail, gaming,
financials, and looking

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.400
at those problems,
which we know already

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.910
that we can solve,
especially in terms

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:25.870
of low latency, high
bandwidth, anywhere,

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:27.650
we know that we are
really good at it.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:29.500
But how do we
bundle that together

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:34.060
and start offering them as a
networking secure services,

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:38.470
which means integrating with
our IAM, giving our visibility

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:40.750
and monitoring all the
way to stack drivers

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:43.030
and also giving
one platform APIs.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:45.190
So to be honest,
really networking

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:47.200
which crosses the
horizontal domain,

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.270
how do you package that
together, and really

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:51.730
start bringing large
enterprises together,

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:54.430
and then show them
what our value add is?

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:57.520
Because networking seems
easy and that's what we want,

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:58.970
but hard to explain.

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:01.990
So being able to explain them
so they can visualize that,

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:03.520
I guess that's
where the problem.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:05.020
Simple problem to solve.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:06.250
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Indeed.

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:07.625
Thank you for your
time, Lakshmi.

00:06:07.625 --> 00:06:08.830
LAKSHMI SHARMA: Thank you.

